# Beat my track launch compliance plan

Prepared 24 October 2026; refined 25 October 2026 after the reviews and publisher's ten answers. The publisher has confirmed a minimum age of **13** and initial distribution in the **EU, US and Canada**. Publisher identity/country/contact details remain unresolved. Finnish governing-law wording is still a draft choice subject to final regional review; it does not remove mandatory protections in other target markets.

The next deliverable is one coherent release candidate with working legal, privacy, purchase and moderation flows. The current [terms revision 6](TERMS_DRAFT.md) and [publisher decision record](PUBLISHER_DECISIONS_2026_10_25.md) supersede the earlier permanent-product and automatic-restoration proposals in the historical [first-review record](LEGAL_REVIEW_RESPONSE.md) and [second-review response](SECOND_LEGAL_REVIEW_RESPONSE.md). They are not in force. The [checklist](LAUNCH_CHECKLIST.csv) separates publisher decisions, engineering, legal review and release evidence so a draft cannot be mistaken for completed compliance.

## Protection against disputes

A blanket “all bugs accepted, no responsibility for anything” clause would not provide the requested protection for Finnish/EU consumer distribution. Unfair terms and mandatory digital-content remedies remain applicable. Calling a build free, beta or early access does not by itself remove those duties; relevant consumer rules can also apply where the consumer supplies personal data for purposes beyond supplying the service. [KKV unfair terms](https://www.kkv.fi/en/consumer-affairs/contracts/unfair-contract-terms/), [digital content](https://www.kkv.fi/en/consumer-affairs/digital-content-and-services/).

The draft limits promises about uninterrupted availability, absence of bugs, universal compatibility, cheat prevention and competitive outcomes. It also gives specific grounds for updates, moderation and discontinuation, and limits liability for appropriate categories of loss where lawful. It preserves mandatory remedies and avoids an arbitrary monetary cap, consumer indemnity, exclusive Finnish court clause, forced arbitration or automatic forfeiture of all paid benefits after a ban. Those additions could create unfairness rather than dependable protection.

Disclose a particular known limitation before acquisition or purchase. If it is a departure from objective conformity requirements that the law permits the player to accept, obtain express separate acceptance. A general terms checkbox or a long list of unspecified bugs is insufficient. Article 8(5), change rules in Article 19 and mandatory effect in Article 22 of [Directive 2019/770](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/770/oj/eng) frame this review; Finnish implementing law and the actual transaction control the result.

Keep the accepted terms version, purchase description, provider transaction and support decision so the business can explain what was agreed and delivered. Collect only the evidence needed under an approved retention policy; a desire to defend disputes is not a licence for indefinite personal-data retention.

## Online service discontinuation

The publisher requires the ability to end online operation and has now chosen operational/commercial wording instead of a consumer-facing list of personal reasons. Section 14 identifies legitimate commercial unsustainability, unsupportable operating/maintenance costs and inability to continue necessary arrangements, subject to existing obligations and mandatory remedies. This confirms a draft policy, not a request to shut down a service.

The publisher also explicitly requires geographical withdrawal where regulation makes a territory too difficult to support. Revision 4 extends section 14 to lawful, proportionate suspension, restriction or discontinuation of game distribution and/or services in specified countries or regions, while operation may continue elsewhere. The reason may be unlawful continued operation or compliance costs/complexity that are no longer reasonably sustainable, including changed interpretation or enforcement; it need not be a complete legal prohibition.

Make that limit visible before acquisition and purchase, particularly beside timed ad-free and publishing-capacity offers. The publisher has replaced the proposed permanent product with a twelve-month non-renewing benefit. A year of paid access still creates commitments for that period; record how affected purchases will be honoured or remedied if supply ends early, and do not retroactively narrow any benefit already sold. A general shutdown reservation does not remove required notice or mandatory consumer remedies. [KKV unfair terms](https://www.kkv.fi/en/consumer-affairs/contracts/unfair-contract-terms/).

Prepare a shutdown procedure covering advance notice and the effective date, stopping sales of benefits that cannot be supplied, outstanding orders and paid entitlements, available local play, required content retrieval, personal-data cleanup/retention and a remaining complaints contact. The publisher has chosen **at least 60 days' in-game notice** for planned global/regional withdrawal, with any longer period/additional notice form required by law. Implement persistent dated notices and an accessible retained copy where needed. Urgent legal/security exceptions need specific reasons; ordinary commercial decisions are not automatically emergencies. Mandatory update obligations must also be assessed when maintenance ends. [KKV digital-content obligations](https://www.kkv.fi/en/consumer-affairs/digital-content-and-services/).

For regional withdrawal, identify the affected territory and objective reason, assess the applicable contract and cross-border access rules, and distinguish stopping new downloads/registrations or purchases from terminating existing access. Coordinate store availability with lawful backend restrictions and staged sales cutoff; a store delisting alone does not close existing accounts. Explain the scope, effective date and any known suspension duration to affected players, handle paid benefits and required retrieval, keep lawful support/privacy routes accessible, and avoid treating a territorial restriction as player misconduct. Define proportionate territory determination, handling of travel/mistakes and data minimisation before implementing access controls. No regional blocking or distribution change is authorised by this drafting instruction.

EU rules distinguish access to online interfaces from conditions of service supply; copyright-content services do not have a blanket exemption from every geographical restriction rule. Review the actual restriction instead of assuming regulatory burden automatically permits any IP/address block. [Commission geo-blocking explanation](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/10-key-features-geo-blocking-regulation).

## Sale succession and major gameplay changes

The publisher also requires the ability to sell or license the game and transferable IP, appoint a different maintainer/developer/operator, and make substantial gameplay changes. Section 19 reserves the relevant business/IP rights while distinguishing them from assignment of player contracts. Section 7 now reserves continuing published-track use, adaptation, promotion and commercial exploitation within Beat my track, future versions/platforms and related game services, including successor use within the same limits. It does not claim ownership of players' protected original contributions or authorise unrelated sales, advertising or AI training. Private submissions retain narrower feature permissions.

An IP sale, subcontracting arrangement and transfer of a player contract are legally different actions. Audit ownership and third-party licences before a transaction, identify which obligations the successor takes on, and obtain any legally required notice/agreement. Do not assume a buyer automatically inherits every asset licence, provider account, consent or permission, or that a sale releases the seller from existing claims. The unfair-terms rules specifically identify transfers that can reduce consumer guarantees without agreement as a potential unfair term. [Directive 93/13 Annex 1(p)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A31993L0013).

Plan any data handover separately: controller/processor roles, lawful basis, necessary records, secure migration, applicable cross-border safeguards, privacy notices and preservation of deletion/consent/ownership restrictions. The general terms cannot turn acceptance into blanket permission for unrelated data uses. [Commission controller and processor guidance](https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/application-gdpr_en).

Section 13 now expressly includes substantial changes to handling, controls/cameras, tracks, rules/formats, scoring/rankings, progression/rewards, presentation, editor and online features. It specifies development, balance, feedback, security, compatibility and evidenced sustainability reasons. Assess each actual change against its reason, what customers were promised, paid rights and the notice/termination requirements. A substantial negative impact can trigger statutory remedies even when the change clause exists. Preserve an unchanged conforming service where that is the lawful route chosen, or provide the applicable termination/remedy route. [KKV modification rules](https://www.kkv.fi/en/consumer-affairs/digital-content-and-services/).

This instruction authorises drafting these future contractual provisions. It does not change the current runtime, canonical driving contract, product invariants or existing player agreements, and does not authorise an actual sale, transfer of records or deployment. A successor must review the applicable contracts rather than treating this draft as a completed assignment.

